    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find \\( 4^4 \\cdot 5^4 \\), we apply the rule of exponents which states that \\( a^m \\cdot b^m = (a \\cdot b)^m \\). Here, we have \\( 4^4 \\cdot 5^4 \\). This can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n(4 \\cdot 5)^4\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the product inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 5 = 20\n\\]\n\nNext, we raise 20 to the power of 4:\n\n\\[\n20^4 = (2 \\cdot 10)^4\n\\]\n\nNow, we further break down the expression:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\cdot 10)^4 = (2^1 \\cdot 10^1)^4 = (2^1)^4 \\cdot (10^1)^4\n\\]\n\nWe use the property of exponents \\((a \\cdot b)^m = a^m \\cdot b^m\\):\n\n\\[\n(2^1 \\cdot 10^1)^4 = 2^4 \\cdot 10^4\n\\]\n\nWe calculate \\( 2^4 \\) and \\( 10^4 \\):\n\n\\[\n2^4 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 16\n\\]\n\\[\n10^4 = 10 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 10 = 10,000\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n2^4 \\cdot 10^4 = 16 \\cdot 10,000 = 160,000\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{160000}\n\\]",
